1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:02,816
Look at your screen.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,925
It seems perfectly still.

3
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:06,547
It isn't.

4
00:00:07,367 --> 00:00:12,167
Up to 120 times every second,
every pixel on it is refreshed.

5
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:17,148
And the moment anything moves, your
Mac has to paint a brand-new picture —

6
00:00:17,148 --> 00:00:20,539
nearly six million pixels —
in about eight milliseconds.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,617
To pull that off, it uses a
processor built for one thing:

8
00:00:24,617 --> 00:00:28,296
doing the same simple math, on
millions of things, at once.

9
00:00:28,667 --> 00:00:30,309
And then it turns electrons…

10
00:00:30,309 --> 00:00:31,248
back into light.

11
00:00:32,167 --> 00:00:34,164
Today, we follow a single frame.

12
00:00:34,164 --> 00:00:34,976
From numbers…

13
00:00:34,976 --> 00:00:35,537
to light.

14
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:48,120
Get close enough to any screen,
and the illusion breaks.

15
00:00:48,633 --> 00:00:51,432
It's a grid of tiny dots, called pixels.

16
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:55,140
A 14-inch MacBook Pro has
nearly six million of them.

17
00:00:55,633 --> 00:01:00,604
And each pixel is really three: a red,
a green, and a blue light, side by side.

18
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,137
Turn all three up, and you see white.

19
00:01:03,137 --> 00:01:04,513
Turn them all off: black.

20
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:09,290
Every color you've ever seen on a screen
is just a different mix of these three

21
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:09,679
lights.

22
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,580
To the computer, a pixel is just three
numbers: how bright the red, the green,

23
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:16,931
and the blue should be.

24
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:21,266
With eight bits per color,
each light gets 256 levels.

25
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:25,815
Multiply them together, and that's
over sixteen million colors.

26
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:30,543
The best Mac displays go further, with
ten bits per color: over a billion.

27
00:01:31,033 --> 00:01:34,937
A whole screen of these numbers
is called a frame buffer.

28
00:01:34,937 --> 00:01:38,841
At four bytes per pixel, one
frame is about 24 megabytes.

29
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:44,887
At 120 frames a second, that's nearly
three gigabytes of pixels, every second.

30
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,445
So who fills in all those numbers…

31
00:01:47,445 --> 00:01:48,017
that fast?

32
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:54,829
Meet the GPU: the
graphics processing unit.

33
00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:58,250
A CPU is like a handful
of brilliant artists.

34
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:02,203
Each one can paint anything —
but they work stroke by stroke.

35
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:05,932
A GPU is an army of thousands
of simpler painters.

36
00:02:05,932 --> 00:02:11,197
They all follow the same instructions,
but each one works on a different pixel.

37
00:02:11,633 --> 00:02:16,452
For pictures, where every pixel needs
the same kind of math, the army wins.

38
00:02:16,452 --> 00:02:17,159
Every time.

39
00:02:18,567 --> 00:02:23,281
On Apple silicon, the GPU sits
right on the same chip as the CPU.

40
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:27,599
Each GPU core packs 128
tiny arithmetic units.

41
00:02:27,599 --> 00:02:32,900
They work in squads of 32 called
SIMD groups: one instruction,

42
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:35,123
thirty-two pixels at once.

43
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:40,729
An eight-core Apple GPU can keep nearly
twenty-five thousand threads in flight at

44
00:02:40,729 --> 00:02:41,616
the same time.

45
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:45,620
Apps talk to it through Metal,
Apple's graphics framework.

46
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:50,050
They pack commands — like “draw these
triangles” — into a command buffer,

47
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:51,081
and hand it over.

48
00:02:51,533 --> 00:02:54,627
So what does the GPU
actually do with a triangle?

49
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:01,327
Meet the most famous teapot in computing.

50
00:03:01,327 --> 00:03:05,068
Graphics researchers have
been drawing it since 1975.

51
00:03:05,567 --> 00:03:09,556
To the GPU, it's nothing but
triangles — thousands of them,

52
00:03:09,556 --> 00:03:12,735
each defined by three
corners, called vertices.

53
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,000
Step one: a small program called a
vertex shader runs once for every corner.

54
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:23,076
It moves, rotates and scales each point,
then projects it onto your flat screen,

55
00:03:23,076 --> 00:03:24,807
so nearer things look bigger.

56
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:27,910
Step two: rasterization.

57
00:03:27,910 --> 00:03:32,436
The GPU works out exactly which
pixels each triangle covers.

58
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:37,722
Step three: for every one of those pixels,
a fragment shader decides its color —

59
00:03:37,722 --> 00:03:40,495
from the light, the
material, and the texture.

60
00:03:40,933 --> 00:03:43,710
Millions of pixels per
frame, all in parallel.

61
00:03:43,710 --> 00:03:46,608
That's what those thousands
of painters are for.

62
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,063
Apple's GPUs add a clever trick.

63
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:55,404
They split the screen into small tiles
— say, thirty-two by thirty-two pixels —

64
00:03:55,404 --> 00:03:57,500
and work on them one at a time.

65
00:03:57,933 --> 00:04:02,828
Before shading anything, each tile figures
out which surfaces are actually visible,

66
00:04:02,828 --> 00:04:05,187
and skips everything hidden behind them.

67
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:09,568
Then it does all the work in
fast memory right inside the GPU,

68
00:04:09,568 --> 00:04:12,384
and writes each finished
tile out just once.

69
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,467
It's called tile-based deferred rendering.

70
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:18,197
Less work, less memory
traffic, less power.

71
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,020
But your screen isn't one picture.

72
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:26,693
Every app paints its
own windows, separately.

73
00:04:27,133 --> 00:04:32,390
A system process called the WindowServer
stacks them all together — adding shadows,

74
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:35,240
transparency and blur
— into one final frame.

75
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:38,329
And because your apps, the WindowServer,

76
00:04:38,329 --> 00:04:41,391
and the GPU all share
the same unified memory,

77
00:04:41,391 --> 00:04:43,987
nobody has to copy those pixels around.

78
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:46,597
Timing is everything.

79
00:04:46,597 --> 00:04:49,642
The next frame is painted
into a hidden back buffer,

80
00:04:49,642 --> 00:04:51,809
while the screen shows the front one.

81
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:55,531
When the display is ready for
a new frame, the two swap —

82
00:04:55,531 --> 00:04:57,576
perfectly in sync with the refresh.

83
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,629
Swap at the wrong moment, and you'd see
a torn image: half old frame, half new.

84
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:06,888
On ProMotion displays, the
refresh rate even adapts:

85
00:05:06,888 --> 00:05:12,935
up to 120 hertz when things move, slowing
down when nothing changes, to save power.

86
00:05:16,733 --> 00:05:18,717
Now the finished frame leaves the chip.

87
00:05:19,133 --> 00:05:23,225
A display engine reads it from
memory and streams it, row by row,

88
00:05:23,225 --> 00:05:26,813
over a high-speed link to the
screen's timing controller.

89
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:30,754
The controller switches on
one row of pixels at a time,

90
00:05:30,754 --> 00:05:35,923
while thousands of column wires deliver
a precise voltage to every pixel in that

91
00:05:35,923 --> 00:05:36,181
row.

92
00:05:36,633 --> 00:05:37,598
Sound familiar?

93
00:05:37,598 --> 00:05:41,071
It's the same row-and-column
trick your keyboard uses.

94
00:05:41,071 --> 00:05:45,188
Top to bottom, row after row, and
then it starts all over again.

95
00:05:46,467 --> 00:05:48,296
So what is a pixel, physically?

96
00:05:48,296 --> 00:05:50,008
Let's take the display apart.

97
00:05:50,467 --> 00:05:52,241
At the back: a backlight.

98
00:05:52,241 --> 00:05:54,016
Then a polarizing filter.

99
00:05:54,016 --> 00:05:56,926
A sheet of glass, covered in transistors.

100
00:05:56,926 --> 00:05:59,126
A thin layer of liquid crystal.

101
00:05:59,126 --> 00:06:00,120
Color filters.

102
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,752
And a second polarizer.

103
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:07,197
There's one transistor for every subpixel
— nearly eighteen million of them.

104
00:06:07,197 --> 00:06:12,653
Each one charges a tiny capacitor that
holds its brightness until the next refresh.

105
00:06:14,033 --> 00:06:17,144
The real magic happens
in the liquid crystal.

106
00:06:17,144 --> 00:06:20,945
Its molecules are long and
thin, like microscopic rods.

107
00:06:21,333 --> 00:06:26,073
The first polarizer only lets through
light that vibrates in one direction.

108
00:06:26,073 --> 00:06:29,359
The second is turned ninety
degrees — so on its own,

109
00:06:29,359 --> 00:06:31,381
it blocks that light completely.

110
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:36,401
But apply a voltage, and the crystals
rotate, twisting the light as it passes,

111
00:06:36,401 --> 00:06:38,041
so some of it slips through.

112
00:06:38,467 --> 00:06:40,195
More voltage, more light.

113
00:06:40,195 --> 00:06:45,658
Every subpixel is a tiny light valve,
and its color filter tints it red, green,

114
00:06:45,658 --> 00:06:46,211
or blue.

115
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:49,243
And the light itself?

116
00:06:49,243 --> 00:06:53,021
On a MacBook Pro, it comes
from thousands of tiny LEDs,

117
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:55,907
grouped into zones that dim independently.

118
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:59,086
Dark parts of the picture get dark LEDs,

119
00:06:59,086 --> 00:07:02,359
while highlights can
blaze at up to 1,600 nits.

120
00:07:02,867 --> 00:07:05,963
Inside each LED, it's
electrons, once again.

121
00:07:05,963 --> 00:07:10,678
Pushed across a junction in a
semiconductor, they fall into holes —

122
00:07:10,678 --> 00:07:13,000
and every fall releases a photon.

123
00:07:13,433 --> 00:07:17,447
The size of that fall, the band
gap, sets the light's color.

124
00:07:17,447 --> 00:07:20,524
These are blue LEDs,
made of gallium nitride —

125
00:07:20,524 --> 00:07:23,801
an invention that won the
Nobel Prize in Physics.

126
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:27,939
A special coating turns part of
that blue into red and green.

127
00:07:27,939 --> 00:07:29,533
Together, they make white.

128
00:07:33,333 --> 00:07:37,960
That light crosses the space between you
and the screen, passes through your lens,

129
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:39,371
and lands on your retina.

130
00:07:39,767 --> 00:07:42,620
There, three kinds of
cone cells respond to it.

131
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:45,655
Roughly speaking, they
sense red, green, and blue.

132
00:07:46,067 --> 00:07:50,215
Your brain compares their signals
and creates the sensation of color.

133
00:07:50,215 --> 00:07:54,003
That's why three tiny lights can
fool you into seeing millions.

134
00:07:54,433 --> 00:07:57,586
And a few milliseconds later,
the next frame arrives.

135
00:07:57,586 --> 00:08:01,452
Your brain stitches them together
into smooth, continuous motion.

136
00:08:03,267 --> 00:08:04,568
So, let's rewind.

137
00:08:05,067 --> 00:08:06,883
An app describes a scene.

138
00:08:06,883 --> 00:08:10,515
The GPU turns triangles
into pixels, tile by tile.

139
00:08:10,515 --> 00:08:13,276
The WindowServer composites the frame.

140
00:08:13,276 --> 00:08:16,328
The display engine streams it, row by row.

141
00:08:16,328 --> 00:08:19,670
Transistors set eighteen
million light valves.

142
00:08:19,670 --> 00:08:23,520
And electrons, falling into
holes, pour out as light.

143
00:08:24,133 --> 00:08:25,968
Up to 120 times a second.

144
00:08:25,968 --> 00:08:31,984
Every scroll, every video, every letter
you type — painted in light, just for you.

145
00:08:32,867 --> 00:08:37,155
Next time: what really happens in the
seconds after you press the power button.
